  {
   "title": "Quality factor",
   "body": "$Q_s$ is the inductor's reactance at resonance divided by $R$. At resonance $|V_L| = |V_C| = Q_s|V_s|$: they can be much larger than the source voltage, but are 180° apart and cancel.\n**Example:** $L$ = 10 mH, $C$ = 25.3 nF give $f_0$ = 10 kHz; with $R$ = 20 Ω, $Q_s = 2\\pi\\cdot10^4\\cdot0.01/20$ = 31.4.",
   "formula": "Q_s = \\frac{2\\pi f_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi f_0 C R}"
  },
  {
   "title": "Series RLC as a bandpass filter",
   "body": "Taking the output across $R$ gives $|H| = 1$ at $f_0$ and smaller values on both sides. The half-power frequencies $f_L$ and $f_H$ (where $|H| = 0.707$) define the bandwidth $B = f_H - f_L = f_0/Q_s$. For $Q_s \\gg 1$: $f_H \\approx f_0 + B/2$, $f_L \\approx f_0 - B/2$. Higher $Q$ means a narrower, more selective filter. Drag $Q_s$ (grey: $Q_s$ = 2): the half-power frequencies close in as $Q_s$ grows. They always satisfy $f_Lf_H = f_0^2$, so they look symmetric on the log axis, but for low $Q_s$ the readouts show $f_H - f_0 > f_0 - f_L$ in hertz.",
   "formula": "\\frac{V_R}{V_s} = \\frac{1}{1 + jQ_s\\left(\\frac{f}{f_0} - \\frac{f_0}{f}\\right)}",
   "plot": {
    "x": {
     "min": 0.25,
     "max": 4,
     "label": "f / f_0",
     "log": true,
     "ticks": [
      0.25,
      0.5,
      1,
      2,
      4
     ]
    },
    "y": {
