  {
   "title": "Ideal and second-order filters",
   "body": "Ideal filters pass one band unchanged and block the rest completely: **lowpass**, **highpass**, **bandpass** and **band-reject (notch)**. Real circuits only approximate them. Second-order (RLC) lowpass and highpass filters roll off at **40 dB/decade** instead of 20. $Q_s \\approx 0.707$ gives the maximally flat (Butterworth) response; larger $Q_s$ gives a peak near $f_0$. Output across the series LC pair gives a notch at $f_0$."
  },
  {
   "title": "Pitfalls",
   "body": "Do not mix up the quality factors: $Q_s = 2\\pi f_0L/R$ (series, small $R$ is good) but $Q_p = R/(2\\pi f_0L)$ (parallel, large $R$ is good). $f_0$ is in Hz; $\\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{LC}$ is in rad/s. $B = f_0/Q$ is in Hz. A useful shortcut for the series circuit: $B = R/(2\\pi L)$."
  }
 ],
